import Colean.MutualCmd

/-!
# Help with `F.Rel`: a proposal

A bisimulation step ends in a goal `T.F.Rel R x.dtor y.dtor`. Today `simp` does nothing with it,
nor with such a hypothesis: `T.F.Rel` is a generated inductive with no lemmas, and for a nested
argument its premise is an occurrence structure's relation, `(Occ.ofIso List).Rel (Occ.ofIso List)
R l l'`, with no lemmas either. A user has to apply the constructors of `T.F.Rel` with their
arguments in the right order, and for a nested argument find `Occ.rel_map` or `Occ.rel_intro`.
The one generated tool, `T.F.rel_map` (the images of one layer by pointwise related functions are
related), fits only when both sides are maps of one layer (section 7).

This file proposes a small set of lemmas that turn these goals into the data equations and the
relation at the recursive arguments, so that `simp` does the step. Every lemma is written and
proved here by hand, on concrete types, to show that it holds and how easily. Part I is the
situation today, Part II the proposal, Part III the proofs of Part I again with it, and Part IV
says where each lemma would live and what the generator needs.

The proposal, in order of importance:

1. Per type, generated with `T.F.Rel` (`Gen/Rel.lean`): `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ`, an iff from one step of
   the lifting at two values of one constructor to the equations of their data and the premises
   of `F.Rel.cᵢ` at their recursive arguments, and `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cⱼ`, `False` for two constructors.
   Both `simp`. The same for each type of a `mutual` block and for the `codata` types of a mixed
   block.
2. Once, in `Model/Occ.lean`: `Occ.Rel` through every combinator and leaf of the library (a
   product componentwise, a sum by sides, a function pointwise, a composite as the relation of
   the relation, `List` as `List.Forall₂`, `Option` as `Option.Rel`), and three lemmas for any
   structure: a map on either side moves into the relation, and a value related to itself is a
   relation at its members. All `simp`. Theorems only, no new definition.
3. Per type, generated: `T.dtor_injective` and `T.dtor_inj`, from a generic
   `Contract.dtor_injective` (`Framework.lean`). They give the unfolding equations of corecursive
   definitions by `simp`, without `conv`.
4. Per declared type, generated by step 5 with `T.Poly.map_cᵢ` and `T.Poly.mem_cᵢ`:
   `T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ` and `T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cⱼ`, the relation of its structure as a leaf on
   constructors, for nesting through a declared type and through the inner types of a mixed
   block.
5. Optional: `T.F.Rel.mono`, `T.F.rel_refl` and the coinduction up to equality `T.coinduct`.

How the file is checked: everything compiles. Where something did not work before the
proposal, the failure is shown by `fail_if_success`, with `simp` run without the lemmas that now
make it work (`simp [-CoList.F.rel_cons_cons]`), with a comment.

Status: items 1, 2, 3 and 5 are implemented, under the names proposed: the lemmas of section 8 in
`Model/Occ.lean` (and their indexed versions), `Contract.dtor_injective` in `Framework.lean`, and
per type the lemmas of sections 9, 10 and 12, generated (`Gen/Rel.lean`, `Gen/Framework.lean`,
`Gen/Surface.lean`); Part II shows their statements, Part III uses them. Item 4, the lemmas of
section 11 per declared type, is not generated yet: they are still written here by hand.
-/

universe u v w

namespace RelHelp

noncomputable section

open Codata

/-! ## Part I: today -/

/-! ### 1. A colist

Unfolding a corecursive definition needs `conv`, since `rw [CoList.map, CoList.corec_eq]` would
also rewrite the right-hand side, and a final `rfl`. -/

codata CoList (α : Type u)
  | nil
  | cons (head : α) (tail : CoList α)

def CoList.map {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) : CoList α → CoList β :=
  CoList.corec fun s => match s.dtor with
    | .nil => .nil
    | .cons a t => .cons (f a) t

theorem CoList.map_nil {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) :
    (CoList.nil : CoList α).map f = .nil := by
  conv_lhs => rw [CoList.map, CoList.corec_eq]
  simp

theorem CoList.map_cons {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) (a : α) (s : CoList α) :
    (CoList.cons a s).map f = .cons (f a) (s.map f) := by
  conv_lhs => rw [CoList.map, CoList.corec_eq]
  simp
  rfl

/-! The bisimulation step ends in `CoList.F.Rel R (.cons _ _) (.cons _ _)`, where `simp` stops. The
user applies the constructor, and has to know its arguments: the head once, both tails, then the
premise. -/

theorem CoList.map_map {α β γ : Type u} (f : α → β) (g : β → γ) (s : CoList α) :
    (s.map f).map g = s.map (g ∘ f) := by
  refine CoList.bisim (fun x y => ∃ s, x = (s.map f).map g ∧ y = s.map (g ∘ f)) ?_ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩
  cases s with
  | nil =>
    simp only [CoList.map_nil, CoList.dtor_nil]
    exact .nil
  | cons a s =>
    simp only [CoList.map_cons, CoList.dtor_cons, Function.comp_apply]
    -- The goal is `F.Rel R (F.cons (g (f a)) _) (F.cons (g (f a)) _)`: `simp` makes no progress
    -- without the generated `CoList.F.rel_cons_cons`.
    fail_if_success simp [-CoList.F.rel_cons_cons]
    exact .cons _ _ _ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩

-- The same with a hypothesis: only `cases` takes it apart.
example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a b : α) (x : X) (y : Y)
    (h : CoList.F.Rel R (.cons a x) (.cons b y)) : a = b ∧ R x y := by
  fail_if_success simp [-CoList.F.rel_cons_cons] at h
  cases h
  exact ⟨rfl, ‹_›⟩

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (b : α) (y : Y)
    (h : CoList.F.Rel R .nil (.cons b y)) : False := by
  fail_if_success simp [-CoList.F.rel_nil_cons] at h
  cases h

/-! ### 2. A stream

A type with one constructor is no different. -/

codata Str (α : Type u)
  | cons (head : α) (tail : Str α)

def Str.iterate {α : Type u} (f : α → α) (a : α) : Str α :=
  Str.corec (fun a => .cons a (f a)) a

def Str.map {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) : Str α → Str β :=
  Str.corec fun s => match s.dtor with
    | .cons a t => .cons (f a) t

theorem Str.iterate_eq {α : Type u} (f : α → α) (a : α) :
    Str.iterate f a = .cons a (Str.iterate f (f a)) := by
  conv_lhs => rw [Str.iterate, Str.corec_eq]
  simp
  rfl

theorem Str.map_cons {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) (a : α) (s : Str α) :
    (Str.cons a s).map f = .cons (f a) (s.map f) := by
  conv_lhs => rw [Str.map, Str.corec_eq]
  simp
  rfl

theorem Str.map_iterate {α : Type u} (f : α → α) (a : α) :
    (Str.iterate f a).map f = Str.iterate f (f a) := by
  refine Str.bisim (fun x y => ∃ a, x = (Str.iterate f a).map f ∧ y = Str.iterate f (f a)) ?_
    ⟨a, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨a, rfl, rfl⟩
  -- `rw [Str.iterate_eq]` on the whole goal would unfold the left-hand side twice.
  rw [Str.iterate_eq f a, Str.map_cons, Str.dtor_cons]
  conv => arg 3; rw [Str.iterate_eq, Str.dtor_cons]
  fail_if_success simp [-Str.F.rel_cons_cons]
  exact .cons _ _ _ ⟨f a, rfl, rfl⟩

/-! ### 3. Nested through `List`

The premise of `F.Rel.node` relates the two lists of children by the relation of their occurrence
structure, `(Occ.ofIso List).Rel (Occ.ofIso List) R`. There is no lemma about it: the route is
`Occ.rel_map`, with both lists written as maps of one list. -/

codata Rose (α : Type u)
  | node (a : α) (cs : List (Rose α))

def Rose.map {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) : Rose α → Rose β :=
  Rose.corec fun t => match t.dtor with
    | .node a cs => .node (f a) cs

theorem Rose.map_node {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) (a : α) (cs : List (Rose α)) :
    (Rose.node a cs).map f = .node (f a) (cs.map (Rose.map f)) := by
  conv_lhs => rw [Rose.map, Rose.corec_eq]
  -- Plain `simp` goes too far here, to `∀ t ∈ cs, corec _ t = map f t`.
  simp only [Rose.dtor_node, Rose.F.map_node, Occ.map_list, Rose.ctor_node]
  rfl

theorem Rose.map_map {α β γ : Type u} (f : α → β) (g : β → γ) (t : Rose α) :
    (t.map f).map g = t.map (g ∘ f) := by
  refine Rose.bisim (fun x y => ∃ t, x = (t.map f).map g ∧ y = t.map (g ∘ f)) ?_ ⟨t, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨t, rfl, rfl⟩
  cases t with
  | node a cs =>
    simp only [Rose.map_node, Rose.dtor_node, List.map_map, Function.comp_apply]
    -- `F.Rel R (F.node _ (List.map _ cs)) (F.node _ (List.map _ cs))`.
    fail_if_success simp [-Rose.F.rel_node_node]
    refine .node _ _ _ ?_
    -- `(Occ.ofIso List).Rel (Occ.ofIso List) R (List.map _ cs) (List.map _ cs)`.
    fail_if_success simp [-Occ.rel_list]
    rw [← Occ.map_list, ← Occ.map_list]
    exact Occ.rel_map _ _ _ _ _ _ cs fun t => ⟨t, rfl, rfl⟩

/-! ### 4. Nested through `Option` and a product

The same, with a constructor of `Option` or a pair: nothing applies but `Occ.rel_intro` at the
layers of the structure, or `Occ.rel_prod_intro` and `Occ.rel_hole_intro`. -/

codata OStr (α : Type u)
  | cons (a : α) (t : Option (OStr α))

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a : α) (x : X) (y : Y) (h : R x y) :
    OStr.F.Rel R (.cons a (some x)) (.cons a (some y)) := by
  fail_if_success simp [-OStr.F.rel_cons_cons]
  exact .cons _ _ _ ⟨true, _, _, rfl, rfl, fun _ => h⟩

codata Bin (α : Type u)
  | leaf
  | node (a : α) (p : Bin α × Bin α)

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a : α) (x₁ x₂ : X) (y₁ y₂ : Y)
    (h₁ : R x₁ y₁) (h₂ : R x₂ y₂) :
    Bin.F.Rel R (.node a (x₁, x₂)) (.node a (y₁, y₂)) := by
  fail_if_success simp [-Bin.F.rel_node_node]
  exact .node _ _ _ (Occ.rel_prod_intro _ _ _ _ (Occ.rel_hole_intro h₁) (Occ.rel_hole_intro h₂))

/-! ### 5. Nested through a declared type

`NRose` nests through `CoList`: the premise is `(Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R`.
`simp` computes the map through the generated instance (`CoList.Poly.map_cons`), but nothing
computes the relation, even on constructors. Here two definitions of one tree, with different
states; the colists of children are made maps of one colist by hand. -/

codata NRose (α : Type u)
  | node (a : α) (cs : CoList (NRose α))

/-- The tree with label `a` whose only child is itself. -/
def NRose.single {α : Type u} (a : α) : NRose α :=
  NRose.corec (fun _ : Unit => .node a (.cons () .nil)) ()

/-- The same tree, counting the depth. -/
def NRose.single' {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : NRose α :=
  NRose.corec (fun n : Nat => .node a (.cons (n + 1) .nil)) n

example {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : NRose.single a = NRose.single' a n := by
  refine NRose.bisim (fun x y => x = NRose.single a ∧ ∃ n, y = NRose.single' a n) ?_
    ⟨rfl, n, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨rfl, n, rfl⟩
  simp only [NRose.single, NRose.single', NRose.dtor_corec, NRose.F.map_node, CoList.Poly.map_cons,
    CoList.Poly.map_nil, Occ.map_ofIso_id]
  fail_if_success simp [-NRose.F.rel_node_node]
  refine .node _ _ _ ?_
  -- `(Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R (.cons _ .nil) (.cons _ .nil)`.
  fail_if_success simp
  -- Both colists as maps of one colist, `Occ.rel_map`, then `simpa` to compute the maps.
  have key {X Y : Type u} (R : X → Y → Prop) (x : X) (y : Y) (h : R x y) :
      (Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R (CoList.cons x .nil) (CoList.cons y .nil) := by
    have := Occ.rel_map (Occ.ofIso CoList) (Occ.ofIso CoList) (Occ.ofIso CoList) R
      (fun _ : PUnit.{u + 1} => x) (fun _ => y) (.cons PUnit.unit .nil) fun _ => h
    simpa using this
  exact key _ _ _ ⟨rfl, n + 1, rfl⟩

/-! ### 6. A `mutual` block

The lifting per type, `Tree.F.Rel R₁ R₂`, is the block's lifting at the relations packed by cases.
Its constructors are the block's (`Tree_Forest.F.Rel.node`), with a premise per argument at the
relation of the argument's type. -/

namespace Mutual

mutual
codata Tree (α : Type u)
  | node (a : α) (children : Forest α)
codata Forest (α : Type u)
  | nil
  | cons (t : Tree α) (f : Forest α)
end

def Tree.full {α : Type u} (a : α) : Tree α :=
  Tree.corec (fun () => .node a ()) (fun () => .cons () ()) ()
def Forest.full {α : Type u} (a : α) : Forest α :=
  Forest.corec (fun () => .node a ()) (fun () => .cons () ()) ()

/-- The same trees, with a counter in the states. -/
def Tree.count {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : Tree α :=
  Tree.corec (fun n => .node a (n + 1)) (fun n => .cons (n + 1) (n + 1)) n
def Forest.count {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : Forest α :=
  Forest.corec (fun n => .node a (n + 1)) (fun n => .cons (n + 1) (n + 1)) n

example {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : Tree.full a = Tree.count a n := by
  refine Tree.bisim (fun x y => x = Tree.full a ∧ ∃ k, y = Tree.count a k)
    (fun x y => x = Forest.full a ∧ ∃ k, y = Forest.count a k) ?_ ?_ ⟨rfl, n, rfl⟩
  · rintro _ _ ⟨rfl, k, rfl⟩
    simp only [Tree.full, Tree.count, Tree.dtor_corec, Tree.F.map_node]
    fail_if_success simp [-Tree.F.rel_node_node]
    exact .node _ _ _ ⟨rfl, _, rfl⟩
  · rintro _ _ ⟨rfl, k, rfl⟩
    simp only [Forest.full, Forest.count, Forest.dtor_corec, Forest.F.map_cons]
    fail_if_success simp [-Forest.F.rel_cons_cons]
    exact .cons _ _ _ _ ⟨rfl, _, rfl⟩ ⟨rfl, _, rfl⟩

end Mutual

/-! ### 7. A mixed block

The children of a tree are a finite forest, nested through the open forests: the premise of
`Tree.F.Rel.node` is `(Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)).Rel (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)) R`. The route
is again `F.rel_map` on the layer as a whole, after writing the right-hand side as a map by the
identity, and the match of the step has to be removed by hand first. -/

namespace Mixed

mixed_mutual
codata Tree (α : Type u)
  | node (a : α) (children : Forest α)
data Forest (α : Type u)
  | nil
  | cons (t : Tree α) (f : Forest α)
end

def Tree.relabel {α : Type u} (h : α → α) : Tree α → Tree α :=
  Tree.corec fun t => match t.dtor with | .node a cs => .node (h a) cs

theorem Tree.relabel_id {α : Type u} (t : Tree α) : Tree.relabel id t = t := by
  refine Tree.bisim (fun x y => x = Tree.relabel id y) ?_ rfl
  rintro _ y rfl
  rw [Tree.relabel, Tree.dtor_corec]
  have : (match y.dtor with | .node a cs => Tree.F.node (id a) cs) = y.dtor := by
    cases y.dtor; rfl
  rw [this]
  conv => arg 3; rw [← Tree.F.map_id y.dtor]
  exact Tree.F.rel_map _ _ _ _ fun _ => rfl

end Mixed

/-! ## Part II: the proposal, implemented

The lemmas proposed here were written by hand in this section; they are now in the library or
generated, under the names proposed, and the hand-written versions are gone. Their statements
are shown by `#check`. -/

/-! ### 8. Once, in `Model/Occ.lean`: the relation of an occurrence structure

`Occ.Rel o o' R x y` says that the layers of `x` and `y` have one shape and related contents. The
workhorse is the same statement through `ofC`: then `x` and `y` are substituted away and every
combinator computes by definition. In the library: `Codata.Occ.rel_iff_ofC`,
`rel_iff_exists_map`, `Rel.mono`, the `simp` lemmas `rel_map_left`, `rel_map_right`, `rel_self`,
the combinators `rel_prod`, `rel_sum_*`, `rel_pi`, `rel_comp`, the leaves `rel_list`
(`List.Forall₂`) and `rel_option` (`Option.Rel`), and `rel_hole`, `rel_const`, now `simp`; the
same for the indexed structures (`Codata.IOcc.rel_*`, with `rel_ofOcc` for a plain structure at
one index). -/

#check @Occ.rel_iff_ofC
#check @Occ.rel_iff_exists_map
#check @Occ.Rel.mono
#check @Occ.rel_map_left
#check @Occ.rel_self
#check @Occ.rel_prod
#check @Occ.rel_sum_inl_inr
#check @Occ.rel_pi
#check @Occ.rel_comp
#check @Occ.rel_list
#check @Occ.rel_option
#check @IOcc.rel_ofOcc

/-! ### 9. Per type, with `T.F.Rel`: one step of the lifting

For each pair of constructors `cᵢ`, `cⱼ` of the functor, generated next to `T.F.Rel` and
`T.F.rel_map` (`Gen/Rel.lean`), both `simp`:

* `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ : T.F.Rel R (F.cᵢ ds xs) (F.cᵢ ds' ys) ↔ ds = ds' ∧ prems xs ys`, the data equal
  (one equation per data argument, as `T.cᵢ_inj`, `HEq` where a later argument depends on an
  earlier one) and the premises of `T.F.Rel.cᵢ` at the recursive arguments, unchanged: `R x y`,
  `∀ a, R (k a) (k' a)` under a telescope, `Occ.Rel` for a nested argument (which section 8
  then computes); `True` when there is nothing to say. The two sides share the data in the result
  index and in the types of the recursive arguments (`ITree.F.rel_vis_vis` at one `X`);
* `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cⱼ : T.F.Rel R (F.cᵢ …) (F.cⱼ …) ↔ False` for `i ≠ j`, where the results meet.

The proofs are by cases on the hypothesis in general position (the result indices need not be
patterns) and the constructor. In a block, they are per type, with the relations per type
(`Tree.F.Rel R₁ R₂`), stated as the user sees the lifting; for the outer type of a mixed block,
at the open forests. -/

#check @CoList.F.rel_nil_nil
#check @CoList.F.rel_cons_cons
#check @CoList.F.rel_nil_cons
#check @Str.F.rel_cons_cons
#check @Rose.F.rel_node_node
#check @OStr.F.rel_cons_cons
#check @Bin.F.rel_node_node
#check @NRose.F.rel_node_node
#check @Mutual.Tree.F.rel_node_node
#check @Mutual.Forest.F.rel_cons_cons
#check @Mixed.Tree.F.rel_node_node

/-! ### 10. Per type: equality by the destructor

`dtor` is injective, by Lambek's lemma, for `codata` and `data` alike: `Contract.dtor_injective`
in `Framework.lean` for any contract (and `Data.Contract.dtor_injective`), instantiated per type
with the other derived lemmas (step 3), and per type of a block from `Tₘ.ctor_dtor`. The point
for users: an equation between values of a coinductive type is an equation between their layers,
so the unfolding equation of a corecursive definition is `T.dtor_injective` and `simp`, with no
`conv` and no final `rfl`. `T.dtor_inj` is `simp`, and so is `T.dtor_eq_cᵢ_iff`. -/

#check @Codata.Contract.dtor_injective
#check @CoList.dtor_injective
#check @CoList.dtor_inj
#check @CoList.dtor_eq_cons_iff
#check @Mutual.Forest.dtor_inj

/-! ### 11. Per declared type, with its instances: its relation as a leaf

Nesting through a declared type `T` (here `CoList`, or the open forests of a mixed block) relates
two values of `T` by the relation of its occurrence structure. Step 5 already generates, per
constructor, `T.Poly.map_cᵢ` and `T.Poly.mem_cᵢ`; with them would come `T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ` and
`T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cⱼ`, stated like them on the leaf `Occ.ofIso fun X => T (H X)` at instances of the
`H`s. They are shown here at the identity (`Occ.ofIso CoList`), the form the lifting of `NRose`
mentions. The proof needs nothing but `T.Poly.map_cᵢ`, the injectivity and disjointness of the
constructors, and cases on the witness of `rel_iff_exists_map` at the structure itself.

Not generated yet, unlike the rest of this part: the generator would need the third instantiation
of the instance, at the polynomial functors of the premises' shapes and a universe above both
sides, for every form of constructor of the presentation (split arguments, structures in two
variables, indices). So these stay written by hand. -/

theorem CoList.Poly.rel_nil_nil {X Y : Type v} {R : X → Y → Prop} :
    (Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R CoList.nil CoList.nil ↔ True :=
  iff_true_intro (Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso CoList) |>.mpr ⟨.nil, by simp, by simp⟩)

theorem CoList.Poly.rel_cons_cons {X Y : Type v} {R : X → Y → Prop} (a : X) (b : Y)
    (s : CoList X) (s' : CoList Y) :
    (Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R (CoList.cons a s) (CoList.cons b s') ↔
      R a b ∧ (Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R s s' := by
  rw [Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso CoList),
    Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso CoList)]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨z, hx, hy⟩
    cases z with
    | nil => simp at hx
    | cons p z =>
      simp only [CoList.Poly.map_cons, Occ.map_ofIso_id, CoList.cons_inj] at hx hy
      obtain ⟨rfl, rfl⟩ := hx
      obtain ⟨rfl, rfl⟩ := hy
      exact ⟨p.2, z, rfl, rfl⟩
  · rintro ⟨h, z, rfl, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨.cons ⟨(a, b), h⟩ z, by simp, by simp⟩

theorem CoList.Poly.rel_nil_cons {X Y : Type v} {R : X → Y → Prop} (b : Y) (s' : CoList Y) :
    (Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R (CoList.nil : CoList X) (CoList.cons b s') ↔
      False := by
  rw [Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso CoList), iff_false]
  rintro ⟨z, hx, hy⟩
  cases z <;> simp at hx hy

theorem CoList.Poly.rel_cons_nil {X Y : Type v} {R : X → Y → Prop} (a : X) (s : CoList X) :
    (Occ.ofIso CoList).Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R (CoList.cons a s) (CoList.nil : CoList Y) ↔
      False := by
  rw [Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso CoList), iff_false]
  rintro ⟨z, hx, hy⟩
  cases z <;> simp at hx hy

attribute [simp] CoList.Poly.rel_nil_nil CoList.Poly.rel_cons_cons CoList.Poly.rel_nil_cons
  CoList.Poly.rel_cons_nil

/-! The same for the open forests of the mixed block, which the trees nest through: the parameter
`α` is constant there (its premise is the constant instance), the trees are the hole. -/

namespace Mixed

theorem Forest.Open.Poly.rel_cons_cons {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} {R : X → Y → Prop}
    (t : X) (f : Forest.Open α X) (t' : Y) (f' : Forest.Open α Y) :
    (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)).Rel (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)) R (.cons t f) (.cons t' f') ↔
      R t t' ∧ (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)).Rel (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)) R f f' := by
  rw [Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α)),
    Occ.rel_iff_exists_map _ _ (Occ.ofIso (Forest.Open α))]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨z, hx, hy⟩
    cases z with
    | nil => simp at hx
    | cons p z =>
      simp only [Forest.Open.Poly.map_cons, Occ.map_ofIso_id, Forest.Open.cons_inj] at hx hy
      obtain ⟨rfl, rfl⟩ := hx
      obtain ⟨rfl, rfl⟩ := hy
      exact ⟨p.2, z, rfl, rfl⟩
  · rintro ⟨h, z, rfl, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨.cons ⟨(t, t'), h⟩ z, by simp, by simp⟩

end Mixed

/-! ### 12. Monotonicity, reflexivity, coinduction up to equality

Generated per type (`Gen/Rel.lean`, `Gen/Surface.lean`): `T.F.Rel.mono` by cases and the
constructor (with `Occ.Rel.mono` for a nested argument), `T.F.rel_refl` by `T.F.rel_map` at the
identity, and the coinduction principle up to equality `T.coinduct`, `T.bisim` at `R x y ∨ x = y`:
the relation needs to hold only where the two sides are not already equal. In a block, per type,
with a relation per type. -/

#check @CoList.F.Rel.mono
#check @CoList.F.rel_refl
#check @CoList.coinduct
#check @Mutual.Tree.coinduct

/-! ## Part III: the same proofs with the proposal

Every step below is `simp` with the lemmas of Part II, which are `simp` lemmas; what is left is
the relation at the next states, an existential that `exact ⟨_, rfl, rfl⟩` closes. -/

/-! ### 13. The colist -/

-- Unfolding equations: through the destructor, by `simp`.
example {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) : (CoList.nil : CoList α).map f = .nil := by
  apply CoList.dtor_injective
  simp [CoList.map, CoList.dtor_corec]

example {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) (a : α) (s : CoList α) :
    (CoList.cons a s).map f = .cons (f a) (s.map f) := by
  apply CoList.dtor_injective
  simp [CoList.map, CoList.dtor_corec]

-- The bisimulation step: `simp` leaves the relation at the tails.
example {α β γ : Type u} (f : α → β) (g : β → γ) (s : CoList α) :
    (s.map f).map g = s.map (g ∘ f) := by
  refine CoList.bisim (fun x y => ∃ s, x = (s.map f).map g ∧ y = s.map (g ∘ f)) ?_ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩
  cases s with
  | nil => simp [CoList.map_nil]
  | cons a s =>
    simp [CoList.map_cons]
    exact ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩

-- Hypotheses too.
example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a b : α) (x : X) (y : Y)
    (h : CoList.F.Rel R (.cons a x) (.cons b y)) : a = b ∧ R x y := by
  simpa using h

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (b : α) (y : Y)
    (h : CoList.F.Rel R .nil (.cons b y)) : False := by
  simp at h

/-! Coinduction up to equality, on the classic example: once the first colist is exhausted, both
sides are the same colist, which `R` need not contain. `append` is defined by `partial_fixpoint`,
so its equations are those of the definition. -/

attribute [with_ccpo] CoList

def CoList.append {α : Type u} (s t : CoList α) : CoList α :=
  match s.dtor with
  | .nil => t
  | .cons a s' => .cons a (s'.append t)
partial_fixpoint

@[simp] theorem CoList.nil_append {α : Type u} (t : CoList α) : CoList.nil.append t = t := by
  rw [CoList.append]; simp

@[simp] theorem CoList.cons_append {α : Type u} (a : α) (s t : CoList α) :
    (CoList.cons a s).append t = .cons a (s.append t) := by
  rw [CoList.append]; simp

example {α : Type u} (s t r : CoList α) : (s.append t).append r = s.append (t.append r) := by
  refine CoList.coinduct (fun x y => ∃ s, x = (s.append t).append r ∧ y = s.append (t.append r))
    ?_ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩
  cases s with
  | nil => simpa using CoList.F.rel_refl (fun _ => .inr rfl) _
  | cons a s => simpa using .inl ⟨s, rfl, rfl⟩

/-! ### 14. The stream -/

example {α : Type u} (f : α → α) (a : α) :
    Str.iterate f a = .cons a (Str.iterate f (f a)) := by
  apply Str.dtor_injective
  simp [Str.iterate, Str.dtor_corec]

example {α : Type u} (f : α → α) (a : α) :
    (Str.iterate f a).map f = Str.iterate f (f a) := by
  refine Str.bisim (fun x y => ∃ a, x = (Str.iterate f a).map f ∧ y = Str.iterate f (f a)) ?_
    ⟨a, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨a, rfl, rfl⟩
  rw [Str.iterate_eq f a, Str.map_cons]
  simp [Str.iterate, Str.dtor_corec]
  exact ⟨f a, rfl, rfl⟩

/-! ### 15. Nested through `List`

The premise of the step becomes `List.Forall₂`, and Mathlib's lemmas about it finish. -/

example {α : Type u} {β : Type v} (f : α → β) (a : α) (cs : List (Rose α)) :
    (Rose.node a cs).map f = .node (f a) (cs.map (Rose.map f)) := by
  apply Rose.dtor_injective
  simp [Rose.map, Rose.dtor_corec]

example {α β γ : Type u} (f : α → β) (g : β → γ) (t : Rose α) :
    (t.map f).map g = t.map (g ∘ f) := by
  refine Rose.bisim (fun x y => ∃ t, x = (t.map f).map g ∧ y = t.map (g ∘ f)) ?_ ⟨t, rfl, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨t, rfl, rfl⟩
  cases t with
  | node a cs =>
    -- `Rose.F.rel_node_node`, `Occ.rel_list`, then `List.forall₂_map_left_iff`,
    -- `List.forall₂_map_right_iff` and `List.forall₂_same` from Mathlib.
    simp [Rose.map_node]
    exact fun t _ => ⟨t, rfl, rfl⟩

/-! ### 16. Nested through `Option` and a product -/

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a : α) (x : X) (y : Y) (h : R x y) :
    OStr.F.Rel R (.cons a (some x)) (.cons a (some y)) := by
  simpa using h

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a : α) (x : X) :
    ¬ OStr.F.Rel (Y := Y) R (.cons a (some x)) (.cons a none) := by
  simp

example {α : Type u} {X Y : Type v} (R : X → Y → Prop) (a : α) (x₁ x₂ : X) (y₁ y₂ : Y)
    (h₁ : R x₁ y₁) (h₂ : R x₂ y₂) :
    Bin.F.Rel R (.node a (x₁, x₂)) (.node a (y₁, y₂)) := by
  simp [h₁, h₂]

/-! ### 17. Nested through a declared type

On constructors, the generated `CoList.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ` compute the relation of the children. -/

example {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : NRose.single a = NRose.single' a n := by
  refine NRose.bisim (fun x y => x = NRose.single a ∧ ∃ n, y = NRose.single' a n) ?_
    ⟨rfl, n, rfl⟩
  rintro _ _ ⟨rfl, n, rfl⟩
  simp [NRose.single, NRose.single', NRose.dtor_corec]

/-! On a variable, a map on either side moves into the relation and a value related to itself is
a relation at its members (section 8), whatever the leaf: `simp` closes the step of `map id`. -/

def NRose.map {α : Type u} {β : Type u} (f : α → β) : NRose α → NRose β :=
  NRose.corec fun t => match t.dtor with
    | .node a cs => .node (f a) cs

example {α : Type u} (t : NRose α) : t.map id = t := by
  refine NRose.bisim (fun x y => x = y.map id) ?_ rfl
  rintro _ y rfl
  cases y with
  | node a cs => simp [NRose.map, NRose.dtor_corec]

/-! ### 18. The `mutual` block: `simp` closes both steps -/

namespace Mutual

example {α : Type u} (a : α) (n : Nat) : Tree.full a = Tree.count a n := by
  refine Tree.bisim (fun x y => x = Tree.full a ∧ ∃ k, y = Tree.count a k)
    (fun x y => x = Forest.full a ∧ ∃ k, y = Forest.count a k) ?_ ?_ ⟨rfl, n, rfl⟩
  · rintro _ _ ⟨rfl, k, rfl⟩
    simp [Tree.full, Tree.count, Forest.full, Forest.count, Tree.dtor_corec]
  · rintro _ _ ⟨rfl, k, rfl⟩
    simp [Tree.full, Tree.count, Forest.full, Forest.count, Forest.dtor_corec]

end Mutual

/-! ### 19. The mixed block: as through a declared type -/

namespace Mixed

example {α : Type u} (t : Tree α) : Tree.relabel id t = t := by
  refine Tree.bisim (fun x y => x = Tree.relabel id y) ?_ rfl
  rintro _ y rfl
  cases y with
  | node a cs => simp [Tree.relabel, Tree.dtor_corec]

end Mixed

/-! ## Part IV: where each piece goes

| Lemma | Kind | Where | Proof | Priority | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ`, `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cⱼ` (`simp`) | generated, per type and per type of a block | `Gen/Rel.lean`, with `T.F.Rel`; per type `Gen/Surface.lean` | cases in general position, constructor | 1 | done |
| `Occ.rel_iff_ofC`, `rel_iff_exists_map`, `Rel.mono` | theorem | `Model/Occ.lean` | from `rel_intro`, `exists_map_of_rel` | 1 | done, and for `IOcc` |
| `Occ.rel_map_left`, `rel_map_right`, `rel_self` (`simp`) | theorem | `Model/Occ.lean` | `toC_map`, `Sigma.mk.inj_iff` | 1 | done (`rel_self` not for `IOcc`, which has no `Mem`) |
| `Occ.rel_list` (`List.Forall₂`), `rel_option` (`Option.Rel`) (`simp`) | theorem | `Model/Occ.lean` | `rel_iff_ofC`, induction | 1 | done |
| `Occ.rel_prod`, `rel_sum_*`, `rel_pi`, `rel_comp` (`simp`); `rel_hole`, `rel_const` made `simp` | theorem | `Model/Occ.lean` | `rel_iff_ofC` | 2 | done, and for `IOcc` (`rel_compC`, `rel_ofOcc`) |
| `T.dtor_injective`, `T.dtor_inj` (`simp`), `T.dtor_eq_cᵢ_iff` (`simp`) | generic theorem, instantiated | `Framework.lean`, `Gen/Framework.lean`, per type `Gen/Surface.lean` | Lambek | 1 | done |
| `T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ`, `T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cⱼ` (`simp`) | generated by step 5 | `Gen/Derive/Simp.lean`, with `map_cᵢ`, `mem_cᵢ` | `rel_iff_exists_map`, cases, `map_cᵢ` | 2 | not yet |
| `T.F.Rel.mono`, `T.F.rel_refl`, `T.coinduct` | generated | `Gen/Rel.lean`, `Gen/Surface.lean` | cases; `rel_map`; `bisim` | 3 | done |

No new definition is needed: `List.Forall₂` and `Option.Rel` are Mathlib's, everything else is a
theorem about existing definitions. What the generators need:

* `T.F.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ`: the statement is the type of the constructor `T.F.Rel.cᵢ` with its conclusion
  and premises turned into an iff, the primed data replaced by fresh variables with equations
  (`HEq` for an argument whose type mentions an earlier one, as `injEq`). `relCtorType` builds
  almost all of it. For a family with result indices that are not patterns, the proof needs the
  general-position cases of `rel_ofC` (`casesPos`), not plain `cases`.
* The indexed structures `IOcc` need the same lemmas as section 8, for families (`IOcc.rel_*`,
  with `R` at the index of each position). `Occ.rel_array` (through `Array.toList`), and `sigma`
  and `piP` (dependent, with `HEq`) are left out here.
* `T.Poly.rel_cᵢ_cᵢ`: stated on the leaf `Occ.ofIso fun X => T (H X)` with the `H` at instances,
  as `T.Poly.map_cᵢ`; the conjuncts are `Occ.Rel (Occ.ofIso H) (Occ.ofIso H') R` for a field at a
  parameter and the relation of the type itself for a recursive argument. The proof needs the
  witness of `rel_iff_exists_map` in a third instantiation `fun X => T (H₂ X)` at a universe
  above both: with a premise `H₂`, or at the polynomial functor of the premises' shapes.

Two limits remain, both rare in practice:

* Relating two different infinite values of a nested `codata` leaf (`Occ.Rel (Occ.ofIso CoList) R
  s s'` for colists built by different `corec`s) is a coinduction of its own: the route is
  `rel_iff_exists_map` with a witness built by `corec`. A coinduction principle for the
  relation of a leaf would be a theorem on `PolyFix` in `Model/Poly.lean`.
* `simp` cannot choose the witness of a relation `∃ s, x = f s ∧ y = g s`: the step ends with
  `exact ⟨_, rfl, rfl⟩` unless the relation is stated so that `simp` closes it (section 18). -/

end

